From 1c426cce3cb26b3b09e3898932a9e0cbb44afa6e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 00:30:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Route `obj.name op= rhs` through both inline caches
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A compound assignment on a member is the same read-modify-write as
`obj.name++`, and it reached neither inline cache for the same reason:
one assignable index reference served both halves, so the counters
never saw it at all — 0 hits *and* 0 misses, against 199 999 for the
plain assignment beside it.

The read now goes through the read cache and the write through the
store cache, on exactly the terms `TryCreateCachedUpdateKey` already
enforces for `++`: a constant key, an ordinary base, no `super`, no
optional chain, no private name. `objectTemp` in the branch above
already evaluates the base exactly once, which is the only thing the
read and the write have to agree on.

Scoped to the twelve operators `CompoundAssignmentToBinaryOperator`
maps. `&&=`, `||=` and `??=` keep the ordinary reference: for them the
write is conditional on the value read, and a cached store would
perform it unconditionally. That table throwing for all three makes
the exclusion look defensive, but it is not — completing the table and
widening the gate to match makes `o.a &&= 1` against a falsy getter
fire the setter 300 times instead of 0.

`CachedStore` takes the computed value as its last argument, so the
read stays inside the store call and cannot float past the right-hand
side; §13.15.2 reads the old value before evaluating the RHS. The
`+= <literal>` shapes keep the specialized helpers the uncached path
used, so the string/number asymmetry of `+` is unchanged, and both
forms end in the same `JSValue` indexer on a miss, leaving strict-mode
reporting and a refused write's silent failure alone.

`compound-assign-store` goes 0 / 0 to 199 999 read hits and 199 999
store hits. Wall clock on a 20 M-iteration in-function loop, eight
interleaved pairs with only the eligibility call differing: median
paired ratio 0.903, seven of eight the same direction, against a
control (`o.x = o.x + 1`, which already took both caches) at 1.002.
Within one build, `o.x += 1` cost 1.163x the spelled-out form before
and 1.043x after.

37 test cases in PropertyStoreCacheTests, weighted to the order and to
the ways a write can be refused: all twelve operators' values; the old
value read before the RHS, proven with an RHS that overwrites the
property being compounded; an RHS that moves the receiver's shape every
iteration; the three short-circuiting forms neither writing nor
mis-valuing; a refused write still evaluating to the computed value; a
nullish base throwing before the RHS runs; a primitive base and a
getter-only property silently discarding in sloppy mode and throwing in
strict; and nested compound assignments not sharing a base temporary.
Suite: 7 392 tests across 13 projects, 0 failures.

Refs docs/performance-roadmap.md item 2-4.
